    },
    {
      "id": "5f18ec39",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "###\n\nTo find out which packages are installed and ready for use, you can use the `Pkg.status()` command to check the status of the packages:\n\n"
      ],
      "attachments": {},
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": true,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "88721336",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "Pkg.status()"
      ],
      "execution_count": 0,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": false,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false,
          "outputInfo": {
            "isExpanded": false,
            "scrollTop": 0
          },
          "maskInfo": {
            "isMaskVisible": true,
            "isEditorVisible": true,
            "isAutoplayActive": false
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "b4d736e7",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "###  Loading packages\n\n"
      ],
      "attachments": {},
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": true,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "8a3330f4",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "In **Julia**, the `import` and `using` statements are used to load packages and provide access to their specific elements, such as functions, types and variables.\n\nBoth statements allow you to use functions from packages or load them, but there is a slight difference in how they are exported into the current namespace:\n\n* `using` – exports all functions.\n* `import` – requires you to explicitly specify which functions to import.\n\nFor example:\n\n"
      ],
      "attachments": {},
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": false,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "d417ea1c",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "import Base.Math.cos\r\nx = cos(0.5)"
      ],
      "execution_count": 0,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": false,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false,
          "outputInfo": {
            "isExpanded": false,
            "scrollTop": 0
          },
          "maskInfo": {
            "isMaskVisible": true,
            "isEditorVisible": true,
            "isAutoplayActive": false
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "b979f383",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "When using `import`, you must write the package name followed by a full stop `.` before calling a function.\n\nThe code above imports the `cos` function from the `Base.Math` package and calls this function with the argument `0.5`. The `cos` function calculates the cosine of an angle in radians and assigns this value to the variable `x`. Thus, the variable will contain the result of calculating the cosine of an angle of `0.5` radians, which is equal to `0.8775825618903728`.\n\nThe `using` statement is designed to load entire packages, rather than individual functions. The code using the `using` statement would then look like this:\n\n"
      ],
      "attachments": {},
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": false,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "19041ae2",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "using Base.Math\r\ny = cos(0.5)"
      ],
      "execution_count": 0,
      "outputs": [],
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": false,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false,
          "outputInfo": {
            "isExpanded": false,
            "scrollTop": 0
          },
          "maskInfo": {
            "isMaskVisible": true,
            "isEditorVisible": true,
            "isAutoplayActive": false
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "4ca16910",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Thus, `using` is used to load packages and automatically export all their selected names into the current namespace, providing convenient access to the functions and variables of the package. `import` also loads the package, but does not automatically export its names, which helps to avoid potential name conflicts and allows you to explicitly manage the imported elements. Choose the appropriate operator depending on your task.\n\n"
      ],
      "attachments": {},
      "metadata": {
        "name": "Section title",
        "engee": {
          "isDisplay": true,
          "isParagraph": false,
          "isClosedParagraph": false,
          "amountHideCellsInside": 0,
          "codeOutputView": "col",
          "codeOutputHidden": false
        }
      }
    },
    {
